## Introduction

PETadex proteins may contain multiple annotated regions. Combining broad InterProScan annotations with
PETadex HMMER matches provides a catalogue of the sequence regions already detected and identifies
InterProScan hits outside the HMM-matched regions for further investigation.

This analysis reuses completed InterProScan and **hmmsearch** results. It does not launch new scans,
predict structures, or apply disorder, sequence-length, low-complexity or E-value filters.
The input scan reporting thresholds still determine which hits are available.

## Objectives & Methods

1. Read the full-ORF FASTA and validate annotation IDs, lengths and one-based inclusive coordinates.
2. Select the first 1,000 FASTA records for a deterministic test (`TEST_PROTEINS=None` processes all records).
3. Retain every matching InterProScan and HMMER hit in `all_domain_hits.tsv`, including repeats and overlaps.
4. Write `accessory_domains.tsv` containing only InterProScan hits with **zero overlap** with any HMMER alignment
   on the same protein. One shared residue excludes the entire hit from this separate accessory table.
5. Calculate union coverage and export all positive-length unannotated regions separately.
6. Compare overlaps and extract gaps in parallel CPU processes, preserving input ordering in all outputs.

A catalogue row is a reported annotation hit, not necessarily a distinct biological domain.
InterProScan signatures can also represent families or sites. The accessory label is an operational
coordinate rule, not experimental evidence of function. Unannotated gaps are not confirmed domains.
Input parsing and output writing remain serial; GPUs are not used.

